{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Courier New;}{\f1 Arial;}{\f2 Calibri;}}{\colortbl;\red240\green240\blue240;\red0\green0\blue0;}\paperw11906\paperh16838\margl1134\margr1134\margt1134\margb1134\fs28\f2\b WSJT-X UDP → Web App Integration Guide (JS/HTML/PHP)\b0\par\fs22\f2 Version: 1.0  \tab Date: 28 Oct 2025  \parAuthor: ChatGPT (for Oliver Bross / MorseTrainerPro)\par\par\fs24\f2\b Overview\b0\par\fs20\f2 Purpose
This document packages a complete, ready-to-hand-over solution to receive WSJT-X UDP telemetry (Status, Decode, Logged ADIF), show “who WSJT-X is working now” in a browser widget, and automatically log the ADIF to your PHP backend.

Why a bridge?
Browsers cannot listen on UDP. WSJT-X emits local UDP datagrams on port 2237. A tiny companion “bridge” on the WSJT-X machine listens on UDP and forwards parsed JSON via WebSocket (or posts to PHP).

Deliverables
• Node.js UDP→WebSocket bridge (primary, JS-friendly)
• PHP endpoint to store Logged ADIF
• Minimal MariaDB schema
• Drop-in browser widget example (vanilla JS; works in Vue as well)
• Optional: PHP-only UDP listener (Logged ADIF only)
• Optional: Python bridge (py-wsjtx)
• PM2 + systemd unit files
• Test checklist & troubleshooting
\par\par\fs24\f2\b Architecture\b0\par{\pard\li360\fs20\f0 Architecture (high-level)
+------------------+         UDP:2237 (unicast or multicast)         +------------------+
|   WSJT-X (PC)    |  --------------------------------------------->  |  Local Bridge    |
|  Settings->Reporting                                            |    |  (Node.js/Py)   |
|  Address: 127.0.0.1 or 224.0.0.1                                |    +--------+------+
|  Port:    2237                                                  |             |
+------------------+                                              |      WebSocket JSON (ws://localhost:8073)
                                                                  v
                                                         +--------+--------+
                                                         |  Web App (JS)   |----> Optional: POST /api/log-wsjt.php (ADIF)
                                                         |  “Now Working”  |
                                                         +-----------------+
Notes
• Use multicast (224.0.0.1) if you want GridTracker and your bridge to listen simultaneously.
• If the PHP API runs on a server, keep the bridge on the WSJT-X PC and let it POST to your server.
}\par\par\fs24\f2\b WSJT-X Configuration\b0\par\fs20\f2 WSJT-X Settings -> Reporting
1) Enable “Accept UDP requests” (WSJT-X UDP server).
2) UDP Server: Address = 127.0.0.1 (single) or 224.0.0.1 (multicast), Port = 2237 (default).
3) Keep GridTracker or other tools enabled—multicast allows multiple listeners.
\par\par\fs24\f2\b A) Node.js Bridge (recommended)\b0\par\fs20\f2 Primary implementation: Node.js UDP -> WebSocket bridge
This companion service runs on the same PC as WSJT-X (or same LAN). It listens on UDP:2237, parses WSJT-X messages, and broadcasts compact JSON over WebSocket to the browser. It also forwards Logged ADIF to your PHP endpoint.\par{\pard\li360\fs20\f0 Setup (Windows or Linux)
> mkdir wsjtx-udp-bridge && cd wsjtx-udp-bridge
> npm init -y
> npm i ws dgram @pgogas/wsjtx-js node-fetch

Create server.js
}\par{\pard\li360\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 // server.js
import dgram from 'dgram';
import \{ WebSocketServer \} from 'ws';
import fetch from 'node-fetch';
import \{ WsjtX \} from '@pgogas/wsjtx-js';

const UDP_PORT = process.env.UDP_PORT || 2237;
const MULTICAST = process.env.UDP_MULTICAST || '224.0.0.1'; // leave empty to skip multicast
const WS_PORT = process.env.WS_PORT || 8073;
const POST_ADIF_URL = process.env.POST_ADIF_URL || 'http://localhost/api/log-wsjt.php'; // change to your server URL
const POST_TOKEN = process.env.POST_TOKEN || ''; // optional bearer token

const wss = new WebSocketServer(\{ port: WS_PORT \});
const udp = dgram.createSocket(\{ type: 'udp4', reuseAddr: true \});
const parser = new WsjtX();

function broadcast(obj)\{
  const json = JSON.stringify(obj);
  for (const c of wss.clients) \{
    if (c.readyState === 1) c.send(json);
  \}
\}

udp.on('message', async (buf, rinfo) => \{
  try \{
    const msg = parser.parse(buf);
    const payload = \{ ts: Date.now(), rinfo, ...msg \};

    // Fan-out to browser clients
    broadcast(payload);

    // Persist when a QSO is logged in WSJT-X
    if (msg.type === 'LoggedAdif' && msg.adif) \{
      const headers = \{ 'Content-Type': 'application/json' \};
      if (POST_TOKEN) headers['Authorization'] = `Bearer $\{POST_TOKEN\}`;
      try \{
        await fetch(POST_ADIF_URL, \{ method: 'POST', headers, body: JSON.stringify(\{ adif: msg.adif, at: payload.ts \}) \});
      \} catch (e) \{
        console.error('POST to PHP failed:', e.message);
      \}
    \}
  \} catch (e) \{
    // Unknown datagram—ignore silently
  \}
\});

udp.on('listening', () => \{
  const a = udp.address();
  console.log(`UDP listening $\{a.address\}:$\{a.port\}`);
  if (MULTICAST) \{
    try \{
      udp.addMembership(MULTICAST);
      console.log(`Joined multicast group $\{MULTICAST\}`);
    \} catch (e) \{
      console.warn(`Multicast join failed: $\{e.message\}`);
    \}
  \}
  console.log(`WebSocket on ws://localhost:$\{WS_PORT\}`);
\});

udp.bind(UDP_PORT);}\par{\pard\li360\fs20\f0 Run
> node server.js

Optional: PM2 (auto-start)
> npm i -g pm2
> pm2 start server.js --name wsjtx-bridge
> pm2 save

Optional: systemd unit (Linux)
File: /etc/systemd/system/wsjtx-bridge.service
}\par{\pard\li720\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 [Unit]
Description=WSJT-X UDP Bridge
After=network.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/usr/bin/node /opt/wsjtx-udp-bridge/server.js
WorkingDirectory=/opt/wsjtx-udp-bridge
Restart=always
User=wsjtx
Environment=UDP_PORT=2237
Environment=UDP_MULTICAST=224.0.0.1
Environment=WS_PORT=8073
Environment=POST_ADIF_URL=http://yourserver/api/log-wsjt.php
Environment=POST_TOKEN=

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target}\par\par\fs24\f2\b Frontend: Browser Widget\b0\par\fs20\f2 Browser widget (drop-in)
This shows “who we’re working now” from Status frames and forwards Logged ADIF to the PHP endpoint (if you prefer client-side POST as well). Replace localhost if your bridge runs elsewhere.\par{\pard\li360\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 <div id="wsjtxNow" style="font:14px system-ui;padding:8px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:8px;">
  Connecting…
</div>
<script>
(function()\{
  const el = document.getElementById('wsjtxNow');
  const wsUrl = 'ws://localhost:8073';
  let ws;

  function connect()\{
    ws = new WebSocket(wsUrl);
    ws.onopen = () => el.textContent = 'Listening to WSJT‑X…';
    ws.onclose = () => \{ el.textContent = 'Disconnected. Reconnecting…'; setTimeout(connect, 2000); \};
    ws.onmessage = (ev) => \{
      const msg = JSON.parse(ev.data);
      if (msg.type === 'Status') \{
        const dial = (msg.dialFrequency/1e6).toFixed(3);
        const now = msg.txEnabled && msg.dxCall ? `TX → $\{msg.dxCall\} ($\{msg.dxGrid || '—'\}) @ $\{dial\} MHz $\{msg.mode\}` : 'Idle';
        el.textContent = now;
      \}
      if (msg.type === 'LoggedAdif') \{
        // Optionally post from the browser as well:
        // fetch('/api/log-wsjt.php', \{ method:'POST', headers:\{'Content-Type':'application/json'\}, body: JSON.stringify(\{ adif: msg.adif, at: msg.ts \}) \});
      \}
    \};
  \}
  connect();
\})();
</script>}\par\par\fs24\f2\b Backend: PHP Endpoint\b0\par\fs20\f2 PHP logging endpoint
Receives raw ADIF from the bridge and stores both the raw ADIF and parsed key fields. For production, validate Authorization headers and sanitize inputs.\par{\pard\li360\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 <?php
// File: public/api/log-wsjt.php
header('Content-Type: application/json');

// Optional: simple token check
$auth = $_SERVER['HTTP_AUTHORIZATION'] ?? '';
$token = ''; // set same as POST_TOKEN
if ($token && $auth !== "Bearer $token") \{
  http_response_code(401);
  echo json_encode(['ok'=>false,'error'=>'unauthorized']);
  exit;
\}

$input = json_decode(file_get_contents('php://input'), true);
$adif = $input['adif'] ?? '';
$ts   = intval($input['at'] ?? time()*1000);

if (!$adif) \{
  http_response_code(400);
  echo json_encode(['ok'=>false,'error'=>'missing adif']);
  exit;
\}

// Minimal ADIF parse (CALL, BAND, MODE). Keep full ADIF as reference.
function adif_get($adif, $tag) \{
  if (preg_match('/<' . preg_quote($tag, '/') . ':(\\d+)[^>]*>([^<]+)/i', $adif, $m)) \{
    return substr($m[2], 0, intval($m[1]));
  \}
  return null;
\}

$call = adif_get($adif, 'CALL');
$band = adif_get($adif, 'BAND');
$mode = adif_get($adif, 'MODE');

// Store to DB (PDO / MariaDB)
try \{
  $pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=radio;charset=utf8mb4','radio','password', [
    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION
  ]);

  // Store raw ADIF
  $stmt = $pdo->prepare('INSERT INTO wsjtx_adif(ts_ms, adif) VALUES(?, ?)');
  $stmt->execute([$ts, $adif]);

  // History entry
  $stmt = $pdo->prepare('INSERT INTO wsjtx_qsos(call_sign, band, mode, ts_ms) VALUES(?, ?, ?, ?)');
  $stmt->execute([$call, $band, $mode, $ts]);

  echo json_encode(['ok'=>true,'call'=>$call,'band'=>$band,'mode'=>$mode]);
\} catch (Throwable $e) \{
  http_response_code(500);
  echo json_encode(['ok'=>false,'error'=>$e->getMessage()]);
\}}\par\par\fs24\f2\b Database Schema (MariaDB)\b0\par{\pard\li360\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 -- MariaDB schema
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wsjtx_now (
  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  ts TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  dx_call VARCHAR(32),
  dx_grid VARCHAR(8),
  mode VARCHAR(16),
  dial_hz BIGINT,
  tx TINYINT(1),
  de_call VARCHAR(32)
);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wsjtx_adif (
  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  ts TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  ts_ms BIGINT,
  adif MEDIUMTEXT
);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wsjtx_qsos (
  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  call_sign VARCHAR(32),
  band VARCHAR(16),
  mode VARCHAR(16),
  ts_ms BIGINT,
  INDEX(call_sign), INDEX(band), INDEX(mode)
);}\par\par\fs24\f2\b Optional: PHP-only UDP Listener (Logged ADIF)\b0\par{\pard\li360\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 <?php
// File: cli/wsjtx_udp_logger.php
// Purpose: listen UDP 2237 and only capture LoggedAdif frames, then POST to your API
// Run: php cli/wsjtx_udp_logger.php

$socket = stream_socket_server('udp://0.0.0.0:2237', $errno, $errstr, STREAM_SERVER_BIND);
if (!$socket) \{ fwrite(STDERR, "Bind failed: $errstr\\n"); exit(1); \}

while (true) \{
  $pkt = stream_socket_recvfrom($socket, 65535, 0, $peer);
  if ($pkt === false) continue;
  // A minimal heuristic: Logged ADIF packets contain "<EOH>" and "<EOR>"
  if (strpos($pkt, '<EOH>') !== false && strpos($pkt, '<EOR>') !== false) \{
    $adif = $pkt;
    $ch = curl_init('http://localhost/api/log-wsjt.php');
    curl_setopt_array($ch, [
      CURLOPT_POST=>true,
      CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER=>['Content-Type: application/json'],
      CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS=>json_encode(['adif'=>$adif, 'at'=>round(microtime(true)*1000)]),
      CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER=>true,
      CURLOPT_TIMEOUT=>3
    ]);
    curl_exec($ch);
    curl_close($ch);
  \}
\}}\par\par\fs24\f2\b B) Python Bridge (alternative)\b0\par\fs20\f2 Alternative: Python bridge using py-wsjtx (if your backend is Python).\par{\pard\li360\qc\cb1\fs18\f0 # pip install py-wsjtx websockets requests
import asyncio, json, requests, websockets
from pywsjtx.wsjtx import WSJTXListener

POST_ADIF_URL = 'http://localhost/api/log-wsjt.php'

async def main():
    async with websockets.serve(handler, '0.0.0.0', 8073):
        listener = WSJTXListener(udp_port=2237)
        for msg in listener:
            payload = \{"ts": getattr(msg, 'utc_timestamp', None)\}
            await broadcast(payload, msg)
            if msg.__class__.__name__ == 'WsjtxLoggedAdifMessage' and hasattr(msg, 'adif_text'):
                try:
                    requests.post(POST_ADIF_URL, json=\{"adif": msg.adif_text, "at": payload["ts"]\})
                except Exception:
                    pass

clients = set()
async def handler(ws, path):
    clients.add(ws)
    try:
        await ws.wait_closed()
    finally:
        clients.remove(ws)

async def broadcast(payload, msg):
    data = \{"type": msg.__class__.__name__, **payload\}
    for ws in list(clients):
        try:
            await ws.send(json.dumps(data))
        except Exception:
            pass

asyncio.run(main())}\par\par\fs24\f2\b Security & Firewall\b0\par\fs20\f2 Security & Firewall
• Bind the WebSocket to localhost unless you need LAN access; otherwise, add a simple token.
• Allow UDP 2237 inbound on the WSJT-X PC (Windows Defender Firewall or ufw).
• If using multicast, ensure IGMP/multicast is permitted on your NIC/Wi-Fi.
\par\par\fs24\f2\b Testing\b0\par\fs20\f2 Testing Checklist
1) In WSJT-X, enable UDP server; set Address=127.0.0.1, Port=2237.
2) Start the Node bridge: node server.js (watch console for “UDP listening” & “WebSocket on 8073”).
3) Open your web page with the widget; it should say “Listening to WSJT-X…”, then “Idle”.
4) Start a CQ or reply; Status frames should show TX -> CALL (GRID) @ FREQ MODE.
5) Log a QSO in WSJT-X; verify POST arrives in PHP logs and DB wsjtx_adif/wsjtx_qsos updated.
\par\par\fs24\f2\b Troubleshooting\b0\par\fs20\f2 Troubleshooting
• No updates in browser: check that the bridge console shows incoming packets; verify WS URL (8073).
• Nothing received: confirm WSJT-X Address/Port and that firewall allows UDP 2237.
• Multicast join failed: fall back to 127.0.0.1 or enable multicast on the adapter.
• PHP 401: token mismatch—set POST_TOKEN env in systemd/PM2 and in PHP check.
• ADIF not parsed: store raw ADIF only, or expand the regex set for the fields you need.
\par\par\fs24\f2\b Integration Notes\b0\par\fs20\f2 Integration Notes
• Vue/React: wrap the widget in a component; keep a singleton WS connection (avoid multiple sockets).
• Wavelog: either store raw ADIF and import in batches, or map to your existing QSO table schema.
• Extending: you can also react to “Decode” messages (spots) to feed your TX Trainer analytics.
\par\par\fs18\f2 --- End of Document ---\par}